Z||000000||GodSend||09-08-2000||05:02 PM||||   Does anyone have a fastback w/ all the windows tinted (including the ones on the fastback part? ) - Could you post a pic?<P>  Im getting my fastback tinted, and was debating weather or not to tint 3 or 5 windows..<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||09-08-2000||06:40 PM||||unless you want to replace the quarter windows very soon, do not put window tint on them. The adhesive on most tints does not like plastic and causes permanent damage.||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||09-08-2000||07:29 PM||||I replaced my sail panels with tinted panels from Doug Kinney, and I'll tell you, it looks so very damn cool with the tint all around. If you want to have the tint look, I highly recommend talking to Doug. Notice, these panel are actually made with tinted plexi, not film.  Apparently tinting the panels with film is not recommended.<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||007DOUG||09-08-2000||11:27 PM||||I tinted the quarter windows in my 88, and it works.............until you wash the car with a warm engine. The steam rolling out of the grilles just peels the tint right off. I would assume rain will do the same. <P>So, I had tinted quarter windows developed, (approx 20%) The only bad part is the GT logo doesn't show through well. The plastic itself is tinted, thus muting the logo. Instead, I have the windows made without a logo. Then most guys will add some type of vinyl sticker to the area where the logo would have been. <P>If you have excellent looking original GT windows, it would be a shame to rip them off. Plus your looking at another $200 for my set on top of the price you've already paying for the professional tint job.<P><P>------------------<BR>4 Indy pace Cars<BR>84 T-top<BR>84 SE<BR>86 SE V6 153K<BR>2 86 GT's<BR>86 GT 3.4 Pearl White T-top<BR>2 88 GT twins<P>However......<BR><I>"Time is my most precious and most perishable of all my possessions."</I>||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||09-08-2000||11:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 007DOUG:<BR><B><BR>I had tinted quarter windows developed, (approx 20%) The only bad part is the GT logo doesn't show through well. The plastic itself is tinted, thus muting the logo. Instead, I have the windows made without a logo. Then most guys will add some type of vinyl sticker to the area where the logo would have been. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Fellas, this product is excellent! (BTW, Doug, my lazy butt finally put the check in the mail today. Sorry for the delay.)  I am extremely happy with the fit and finish of these panels.  I agree with Doug that it is a shame to take off good panels since it is near impossible not to destroy them in the process, but after 12-14 years, very few original panels are left in good shape. Mine were very bad. The new ones are very good, and I wouldn't dream of messing up the clean look with a decal.||||reg||1
Z||000005||contra||09-09-2000||03:50 PM||||i tinted my GT fastback windows black with 5% tint, and they look amazing.<BR>i had cracks galore on my driverside, and this and that, and they looked all scuffed up. the tint brought them back to life and they look brand new now. it's so dark you can't see the cracks, or the gt logo, but you can still see through the window.<BR>hey, it's better than buying new quarterpanels if you have cracked ones.||||reg||1
Z||000006||007DOUG||09-09-2000||04:30 PM||||Contra:<P>  You must have tinted the outside of your window. I tinted mine from the back side. I thought about your approach, but i didn't figure it would last very long.<P>I've also painted the backside of GT windows before, but creates one heck of a blind spot. I had to add those 1" stick-on convex mirrors to solve the problem.||||reg||12
